/// std/clone: `Clone`, a trait for making an independent, deeply-copied duplicate
/// of a value.
///
/// `Clone` is the dual of the prelude's `Delete`. `Delete` *releases* an owned
/// resource (and, by existing, makes a type move rather than alias); `Clone`
/// *duplicates* one into a second owner that is wholly independent of the first,
/// so destroying either leaves the other intact. For a `Copy` type - an integer,
/// a plain struct - a duplicate is just a bitwise copy, and `clone` returns
/// `*self`. For an owning type, a bitwise copy would alias the heap buffer and
/// double-free it, so `clone` must genuinely re-allocate: `String::clone` copies
/// the bytes, `Vec<T>::clone` allocates a new buffer and clones each element.
///
///     let a: String = String::from_str("hi");
///     let b: String = a.clone();   // a still usable; a and b own separate buffers
///
/// Unlike `Delete`, this is an ordinary trait, not a lang item: nothing in the
/// compiler knows its name, and a clone never happens implicitly. You always
/// write `.clone()`, so every heap copy is visible at the call site - which is
/// exactly the point. Because both the original and the clone are ordinary
/// owners, the compiler's automatic `delete` runs once on each, with no special
/// handling here.
///
/// Implement it for your own types to make them duplicable the same way:
///
///     extend Point: Clone {
///         proc clone(*self) Point { return Point { x: self.x, y: self.y }; }
///     }

import string { String }
import vec { Vec }

pub trait Clone {
    proc clone(*self) Self;
}

// --- scalars
//
// Every scalar is `Copy`, so its clone is the value itself. `str` is a borrowed
// view (also `Copy`), and cloning it yields the same view - copying the *text*
// into an owned buffer is `String::from_str`, a different operation.

extend u8:  Clone { proc clone(*self) u8  { return *self; } }
extend u16: Clone { proc clone(*self) u16 { return *self; } }
extend u32: Clone { proc clone(*self) u32 { return *self; } }
extend u64: Clone { proc clone(*self) u64 { return *self; } }
extend i16: Clone { proc clone(*self) i16 { return *self; } }
extend i32: Clone { proc clone(*self) i32 { return *self; } }
extend i64: Clone { proc clone(*self) i64 { return *self; } }
extend f32: Clone { proc clone(*self) f32 { return *self; } }
extend f64: Clone { proc clone(*self) f64 { return *self; } }
extend bool: Clone { proc clone(*self) bool { return *self; } }
extend str: Clone { proc clone(*self) str { return *self; } }
